# Support and limits

Odroe is evolving in public. The source tree contains the contract suites below;
run them on the revision and platform you intend to use. “Preview” means the
API is usable but the deployment or provider boundary remains narrow. The
presence of a test is not evidence that it passed on every platform.

## SDK versions

The supported minimum is Flutter 3.38.1 with its bundled stable Dart 3.10.0.
Odroe declares Dart `^3.10.0` and Flutter `>=3.38.1`; no dependency overrides
are needed. Flutter 3.38.0 bundles Dart 3.10.0-290.4.beta and cannot resolve a
fresh consumer with that stable Dart constraint. Replacing Flutter's built-in
Dart independently can also break the compiler/engine Kernel SDK pairing.

The minimum SDK workflow verifies normal dependency resolution, analysis,
compiler and CLI contracts, typed RPC and SQLite, Flutter consumption, and a
fresh generated application's persisted state after restarting and relocating
its native server bundle. The current integration workflow uses Flutter 3.47.5
for the broader contract, browser and local provider suites. These checks do
not establish mobile signing, remote deployment, or every target architecture.

## Current status

| Surface | Status | Evidence boundary |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Flutter Web | Verified locally | Build, semantic handoff, Query/RPC application |
| Flutter Android and iOS | Requires separate validation | This integration does not repeat APK or iOS builds; no signing, device, or store-release evidence |
| Native server | Verified locally | Real socket, static files, streaming, shutdown |
| Typed RPC records and enums | Verified | Four-way codecs; nested collections, aliases, nullable values, streams, malformed payloads; VM, Native AOT, and Dart2JS execution |
| SQLite | Verified | CRUD, returning rows, batches, transactions, append-only migrations |
| PostgreSQL | Verified | Real single connection, pool concurrency, transactions |
| MySQL/MariaDB | Preview | Real provider tests require an explicitly configured disposable MySQL/MariaDB database; unit tests alone do not verify either provider |
| Cloudflare Worker + D1 | Preview | Compiled Worker, local Workerd/D1 suite, transient-error classification |
| Remote Cloudflare deploy | Not claimed | Deployment remains application-owned |

## Deliberate boundaries

- application composition can use one `odroe_flutter.dart` import, while
  runtime-specific source still uses narrow entrypoints to keep IO, FFI, and
  JavaScript interop out of the wrong reachable program;
- Odroe's database layer is typed SQL with a focused native SQLite SQL runner,
  not a full ORM or cross-provider migration framework;
- MySQL/MariaDB exposes an owned lazy pool with an explicit connection ceiling,
  bounded pending queue, and queue timeout, but has no portable `RETURNING` or
  conflict-target insert contract. Saturation is `SqlErrorCode.unavailable`;
  the pool does not retry writes or promise one session across top-level calls;
- multi-row inserts are one statement and are not auto-chunked; callers must
  respect provider bind limits. MySQL has no `RETURNING`, and other providers
  do not guarantee that returned rows follow input order;
- D1 exposes atomic batches, not an interactive transaction callback;
- there is no streaming database query surface yet; drivers materialize result
  sets, while typed single-row selects cap the SQL result at two rows;
- browser RPC is same-origin; native apps must provide an explicit HTTP(S)
  origin;
- generated record and enum codecs require a project-local, prefixed source
  with the typedef or enum declared directly in it. Records must be
  non-generic and named-field-only; dependency enums, indirect declarations,
  nominal classes, and values hidden by `Object` or `dynamic` use an explicit
  `SerializationAdapter`;
- local tests do not imply a remote deployment, signed mobile release, DNS
  change, certificate result, or production database migration.

## First diagnostics

```sh
dart analyze --fatal-infos
flutter test
```

For file-route or generated RPC issues, also run `dart run odroe generate`.
Confirm that `.dart_tool/package_config.json` resolves `odroe` to the selected
hosted package or checkout. The optional `init` command intentionally refuses a
transitive dependency, custom Dart source, conflicting configuration, or a
symbolic-link target.

For native RPC, confirm `ODROE_RPC_ORIGIN` is an absolute HTTP(S) origin with no
path, query, fragment, or credentials. For local D1, run the locked sequence in
[First application](/docs/getting-started#run-the-edge-path-locally) and keep
every Wrangler command local.

For a Query type conflict, find every definition using the reported canonical
key. They must share the same exact `QueryKey<T>` data type; do not widen or cast
a typed key to bypass the check. Give keys stored outside `QueryOptions<T>` an
explicit type, and use `QueryKey<Object?>` only for prefix matching.

## Report an actionable issue

Include:

- Odroe commit or package version and the imported entrypoint;
- Dart, Flutter, operating system, Node, npm, and provider versions that matter;
- the smallest route/function/schema that reproduces the problem;
- exact command, exit code, and complete first error;
- whether the failure is analyzer, unit test, native socket, browser, Workerd,
  real database, or remote provider evidence;
- generated target diff only when generation changed unexpectedly.

Do not publish credentials, connection strings, access tokens, private request
bodies, or production data. Open a focused report in the
[Odroe issue tracker](https://github.com/odroe/odroe/issues).
